      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "A survey was performed to obtain information on the organization and practice of postoperative pain management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A questionnaire was mailed to 773 directors of German departments of anesthesiology."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A total of 446 replies (57.7%) could be analyzed. Of the departments, 161 (36.1%) had established an acute pain service (APS), more often in hospitals > or = 1000 beds (63%) than in hospitals with 400-999 beds (40%) and hospitals with < 400 beds (27%). Epidural analgesia was practiced in 97% of the departments, however, it was the analgesic technique of choice for larger abdominal surgery or amputation of the lower limb only in 60.8% and 45.5% of the departments, respectively. Departments with APS provided epidural analgesia more often on general wards than departments without APS (88.2% vs. 68.4%, p < 0.01). Technically more challenging methods (e.g. catheters for regional anesthesia, PCA, PCEA) were more often provided in hospitals running an APS (p < 0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The number of departments with APS has increased over the last 10 years. Future decisions on reimbursement should consider this extensive service."
        }
      ]},
